[DIAGNOSIS]:
The syntax error at line 344, column 5 is caused by the attempt to use "and" to join two top-level quantified formulas within the body of a predicate in Alloy 6. Alloy 6 does not allow an explicit "and" between two statements at the top level of a predicate body; instead, each predicate body must be a single Boolean formula, or use Alloy's block semantics (curly braces) for implicit conjunction. Prior attempts to fix this either used grouping (parentheses/braces), predicate extraction, or quantifier flattening—all of which Alloy 6 still parsed as invalid due to how it parses top-level conjunctions of quantifiers or because the block structure was not correctly used.

[FIX INTENT]:
Transform the predicate into a single return-value function that returns a Boolean expression, thus allowing the conjunction of quantified statements as the function's result expression.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Replace the predicate pred EventualReturnToNormal[et: EmergencyTrigger] with a function fun EventualReturnToNormal[et: EmergencyTrigger]: Bool that returns a single Boolean formula combining both quantified conditions using the "&&" (and) operator.
- Step 2: Write the function’s body as a single return expression: 
      (some s: State | s.session = et.session and TO/lte[s.t, et.endedAt] and s.mode = NormalMode)
      &&
      (all s': State | s'.session = et.session and TO/gte[s'.t, et.endedAt] implies s'.mode = NormalMode)
- Step 3: In the assertion assertR2_EventualReturnToNormal, replace the call to the predicate with a call to the function (i.e., EventualReturnToNormal[et]).

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant because functions must return a single Boolean value and allow multiple quantified formulas to be combined in a single return expression using logical operators; it is different from previous attempts, which did not re-cast the logic as a function or use a return expression.

[LESSON]:
When Alloy 6 predicate bodies cannot accommodate multiple top-level quantifiers joined by logical operators, recast the logic as a function returning a Boolean formula that combines the desired conditions.
```

